    <h2>1. The Problem: The Executive Review Bottleneck</h2>
    <p>
      Across our enterprise digital organization, prototyping velocity was failing to keep pace with how quickly executive leadership needed to weigh in on strategic direction. The issue was not idea generation; it was the friction required to get a <em>real, working artifact</em> in front of decision-makers without:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Waiting weeks on production engineering capacity to stand up throwaway proof-of-concept builds.</li>
      <li>Forcing busy VPs and Directors into synchronous calendar meetings just to gather initial reactions.</li>
      <li>Losing crucial interaction fidelity and motion logic during the handoff from concept to click-dummy.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Leadership needed the ability to review concepts <strong>asynchronously and at enterprise scale</strong>: open a live link on their own schedule, interact with software that genuinely behaves like the shipped product, and leave precise, in-context feedback without a meeting on anyone's calendar.
    </p>

    <h2>2. My Role: Leading with AI Execution</h2>
    <p>
      I led the initiative and treated modern generative AI not as a superficial drafting assistant, but as the primary execution layer for our implementation pipeline. System architecture, component scaffolding, automated test suites, and iteration loops were AI-orchestrated under my direct oversight and design judgment.
    </p>
    <p>
      This division of labor allowed our team to focus purely on craft, interaction ergonomics, and strategic trade-offs rather than plumbing. Crucially, I made the key architectural decisions around brand boundaries, automated governance, and review ergonomics; the AI-augmented build loop gave us the leverage to execute those decisions in days rather than quarters.
    </p>

    <!-- Skill Map Network Diagram -->
    <img src="/images/ai-prototyping/skill-map-arc.png" alt="Arc diagram of interconnected AI capabilities in the prototyping pipeline" class="project-image" loading="lazy" />
