    <h3>B. Multi-Brand Governance: Resisting the False Compromise</h3>
    <p>
      The ultimate test of this capability arrived when we expanded from our core retail app to a second, structurally different product line: <strong>Health100 (Rx App)</strong>. Health100 featured a distinct visual identity, strict clinical compliance requirements, and an embedded AI health assistant.
    </p>
    <p>
      The conventional corporate impulse would have been to force both brands into a single homogenized component system to "avoid duplication." <strong>I made the deliberate leadership call to reject that approach.</strong> Forcing a shared UI system would have caused subtle brand drift and compromised the healthcare experience.
    </p>
    <p>
      Instead, I architected a bifurcated foundation: each brand maintains complete autonomy over its design tokens, components, and interaction schemas, while sharing the underlying, brand-neutral engine underneath (headless state machines, verification gates, and preview servers). This strategic judgment prevented cross-brand pollution while preserving 100% operational reuse.
    </p>

